course: dictionary
lesson: 4118
title: Glamour and its clues
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context shows you which one is meant. «The film premiere had all the glamour of Hollywood, with stars in silver gowns.» Ask: what would fit here? Exciting, stylish and glittering all fit, so glamour means the special attraction of something rich and shiny.
explain: Older stories use glamour in a different way. «The witch cast a glamour, and the beggar looked like a prince.» The clues are cast and looked like: a spell made things seem to be something they were not. When a word surprises you, look for a clue in the sentence that matches its meaning.
explain: Spelling can help too. When a word ends in -our, the u usually drops before -ous, so glamour becomes glamorous, just as humour becomes humorous. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check that it still makes sense.
fact: Glamour means the exciting, special attraction of something that seems rich, stylish or beautiful.
fact: In old stories, a glamour was a magic spell that made things look different from what they really are.
fact: Before -ous, -our usually loses its u: glamour, glamorous; humour, humorous.
fact: The US spelling is glamor, without the u, but the meaning is the same.
fact: Writers use glamour to show that something shiny may hide something ordinary.
exercise: «The film premiere had all the glamour of Hollywood.» What does glamour mean here? | A list of names on a door | A loud noise in the night | A heavy coat for cold weather | *The exciting attraction of something rich and stylish
exercise: «The witch's glamour made the beggar look like a prince.» What is a glamour in this story? | A long walk to the castle | *A magic spell that changes how things look | A kind of expensive cake | A small wooden box
exercise: Change glamour into an adjective meaning full of glamour. Which spelling is correct? | glamour | glamourous | *glamorous | glamorus
test: «Ella said the shiny cafe had a touch of glamour, but the food was ordinary.» What might the author be suggesting? | The cafe was closed all day | The food was the best in town | *Something shiny may hide something ordinary | Ella loves cafes with no windows
test: Which phrase means adding a little attraction and excitement? | *add a touch of glamour | take away the lights | add a cup of flour | close the door quietly
exam: «The glamorous actress smiled, but the glamour faded when the lights went out.» What does glamour most likely mean at the end? | *The exciting attraction that seems special | A kind of soup | A wet, grey day | A small animal
